1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:05,840
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,560
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,050
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,440
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:16,400
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:19,920
Today, our topic is asking for help.

8
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:22,140
And why we do not.

9
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:25,900
Ben, when did you last ask someone for help?

10
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:27,600
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:28,480
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:30,200
Yesterday, Mia.

13
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:34,120
I asked my daughter to find a file on my phone.

14
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,160
That is the size of my asks.

15
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,020
Small, and to a seven-year-old.

16
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:43,040
The big ones, I do not ask.

17
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:44,460
What about you?

18
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:46,840
I have a box, Ben.

19
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:48,260
That is my story.

20
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:51,340
There was a box on the top shelf of my kitchen.

21
00:00:51,769 --> 00:00:52,440
A heavy one.

22
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:53,780
Winter things.

23
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:55,500
Coats, boots.

24
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,380
And for one year, I could not reach it.

25
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:00,060
I stood on a chair.

26
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:01,540
I used a broom.

27
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,560
I almost fell, twice.

28
00:01:04,239 --> 00:01:05,880
Once with the box in my hand.

29
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:07,660
A year, Mia?

30
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,370
On a chair, with a broom, in your own kitchen?

31
00:01:12,210 --> 00:01:12,840
And your neighbor?

32
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,110
The tall one from our hello episode?

33
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:18,300
On the other side of the wall, Ben.

34
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:19,190
The whole year.

35
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,280
Two meters tall.

36
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:22,440
And I never asked.

37
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:24,580
Because I had a picture in my head.

38
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:25,660
I knock.

39
00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:27,020
He opens the door.

40
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:29,590
And his face says, "Really?

41
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:30,660
For a box?

42
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,140
I am busy." And I feel small, and I go back to my chair.

43
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:42,310
"I feel small, and I go back to my chair." Mia, that is our topic today.

44
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:44,280
So here is our plan.

45
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:49,400
In part one, we talk about the things we do not ask for.

46
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:55,560
In part two, we ask why asking feels so bad, and what really happens when we ask.

47
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,320
And I have a true story for that.

48
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:03,040
In part three, we share simple ways to ask for help this week.

49
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:04,660
Does that sound good?

50
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:06,660
That sounds good, Ben.

51
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:10,979
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

52
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:12,340
You will hear it again.

53
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,040
And if you do not understand a word, ask someone.

54
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:17,600
That is the topic.

55
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:18,940
Okay, Ben.

56
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:19,720
Part one.

57
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,730
Do you want to know what happened when I finally knocked?

58
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:24,920
Tell me, Mia.

59
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,720
What did the face do when you finally knocked?

60
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,760
Was it the busy face?

61
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:31,300
I need to know.

62
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,260
It lit up, Ben.

63
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:35,000
He said yes before I finished the sentence.

64
00:02:35,549 --> 00:02:36,300
"Of course!

65
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:41,670
Why did you not ask before?" And he got the box in ten seconds, without a chair.

66
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:44,340
And then he asked if I needed anything else.

67
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:51,300
And then he told me about his own high shelf, and how his back hurts, and could I help him with something next week.

68
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:53,020
His shelf, my ladder.

69
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:56,240
He asked you for help back, Mia.

70
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:57,190
In the same minute.

71
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,980
And "why did you not ask before?" He was almost hurt, wasn't he?

72
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:04,000
That you did not ask.

73
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:07,140
He asked me back in the same minute, Ben.

74
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,020
Is that normal?

75
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:10,470
Do people who help want help?

76
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,620
I think asking opens a door both ways, Mia.

77
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:16,780
When you asked, you told him it was allowed.

78
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:17,650
So he asked.

79
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:22,520
People are waiting for permission, like in our hug episode.

80
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,460
Your box gave him permission for his back.

81
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,980
One ask, two people helped.

82
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:28,940
A little, Ben.

83
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,200
And I did not expect that at all.

84
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:34,280
In my picture, asking was a bother.

85
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,740
In his face, not asking was the bother.

86
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:42,950
He had been on the other side of the wall for a year, and I had never needed him.

87
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:45,680
As far as he knew.

88
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:48,040
The box was never about the box.

89
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:50,640
The face did the opposite of the picture, Mia.

90
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:53,520
And I have a broom, too.

91
00:03:53,530 --> 00:03:57,990
At work, for two years, I did not know how to use one part of our system.

92
00:03:58,620 --> 00:03:59,920
Two years.

93
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:02,100
I did it the slow way, every day.

94
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:04,620
One hour instead of five minutes.

95
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:09,100
My box was a computer, and my broom was a spreadsheet.

96
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:11,240
Two years, Ben?

97
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,700
An hour a day, for two years?

98
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:15,600
Why did you not ask?

99
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:17,040
Nobody?

100
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:20,740
Because after month one, asking felt too late, Mia.

101
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:25,960
"You have been here a year and you do not know this?" That was the picture.

102
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:27,320
So I hid it.

103
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:30,090
And then a new person started.

104
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:32,060
And on day one, she asked.

105
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:36,280
"How does this work?" And someone showed her in five minutes.

106
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,600
Five minutes, Ben.

107
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:41,400
And you did the hour for two years.

108
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:44,220
So the new person knew something you forgot.

109
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:47,020
She knew that day one is free, Mia.

110
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:50,160
On day one, nobody expects you to know.

111
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:51,420
So you ask.

112
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:56,920
And then week two, month two, year two, the asking gets heavier.

113
00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:58,920
Like the silence in the hallway.

114
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:02,000
Two years of hiding a five-minute question.

115
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,120
The best time to ask is day one.

116
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:06,760
The second best is today.

117
00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:10,040
Day one is free, Ben.

118
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,120
And I am on day one thousand with some questions.

119
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:16,220
Is there a way to get day one back?

120
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:17,380
Yes, Mia.

121
00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:19,279
You say the words out loud.

122
00:05:20,189 --> 00:05:22,200
"I should know this, and I do not.

123
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:25,380
Can you show me?" Honest, and short.

124
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:27,380
People do not laugh at that.

125
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,960
People say, "Of course." The same face as your neighbor.

126
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:33,460
Honesty makes it day one again.

127
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:34,980
It resets the clock.

128
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:37,920
"The best time to ask is day one.

129
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:43,240
The second best is today." Ben, that is the hello episode again.

130
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:47,300
And I think the word "help" is part of the problem.

131
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:48,620
We make it too big.

132
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:50,340
Too big how, Mia?

133
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:51,900
Help is help.

134
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:54,040
You need it, or you do not.

135
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:55,140
What do you mean?

136
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,180
Help sounds like rescue, Ben.

137
00:05:58,220 --> 00:05:59,740
Like a fire.

138
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:02,620
And nobody wants to call the fire truck for a box.

139
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:04,980
A box is not a fire.

140
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:07,480
But most help is tiny.

141
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:08,740
Hold this.

142
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:09,720
Look at this.

143
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:11,100
Say that again.

144
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:12,420
Reach that.

145
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:18,400
Help is a hundred small grey things, and I thought it was only for emergencies.

146
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:19,819
Red things.

147
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:27,030
"Help is a hundred small grey things." Mia, so your daily life had no help in it at all.

148
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:29,200
Everything alone, every day.

149
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:30,610
And alone is not strong.

150
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:33,560
Alone is just slow.

151
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:35,040
Okay, so that was part one.

152
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:36,140
Our brooms.

153
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:37,820
Now, part two.

154
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:39,980
Why does asking feel so bad?

155
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,100
And what really happens?

156
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:42,740
I have a true story.

157
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:45,140
A true story about asking, Ben?

158
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:46,800
Somebody measured this?

159
00:06:47,190 --> 00:06:49,700
With real strangers, on a real street?

160
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:51,180
Two people did, Mia.

161
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:52,779
Okay, part two.

162
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:54,880
This is a true story.

163
00:06:54,940 --> 00:07:01,010
In 2008, Francis Flynn and Vanessa Bohns, at Columbia University in New York, did a simple experiment.

164
00:07:01,590 --> 00:07:04,900
They asked students to go out and ask strangers for small things.

165
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:06,240
Fill in a survey.

166
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:08,180
Lend me your phone for a call.

167
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:09,760
Walk me to the gym.

168
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:12,060
Walk me to the gym, Ben?

169
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:14,110
To a stranger, on the street?

170
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:15,360
I would never.

171
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:16,069
Never.

172
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:17,660
Neither would I, Mia.

173
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:22,180
But first, before the students went out, the researchers asked them to guess.

174
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:27,300
"How many people will you need to ask, to get ten yeses?" And the students guessed.

175
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:31,060
And then they went out, and they asked people, and they counted.

176
00:07:31,630 --> 00:07:32,940
And the guess was wrong, Ben?

177
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,340
I already know the answer, I think.

178
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:36,700
But tell me anyway.

179
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:37,820
Slowly.

180
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:39,519
Wrong by half, Mia.

181
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:44,300
The students thought they would need to ask about twenty people to get ten yeses.

182
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:46,580
In reality, they needed about ten.

183
00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:50,840
People said yes twice as often as the students expected.

184
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:51,860
For every task.

185
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:54,570
The survey, the phone, the walk.

186
00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:56,540
Twice as often.

187
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:57,880
Twice as often, Ben.

188
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:02,800
So the picture in my head, the busy face, was wrong by half.

189
00:08:02,830 --> 00:08:06,900
And the real face at the door, the one that said yes, was the other half.

190
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:08,940
Two different worlds, Mia.

191
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,150
The asker's world, where asking is a big frightening thing.

192
00:08:12,770 --> 00:08:16,760
And the asked person's world, where it is a small normal thing.

193
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:21,040
The asking feels terrible to the asker, and normal to the asked.

194
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:24,300
A box is heavy for one and light for the other.

195
00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:32,080
"The asking feels terrible to the asker, and normal to the asked." Ben, I was living in the asker's world for a year, on a chair.

196
00:08:32,790 --> 00:08:34,980
And my neighbor was in the other world, waiting.

197
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:38,080
Why are the two worlds so different?

198
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:40,559
Because saying no is hard, too, Mia.

199
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:42,000
That is what the researchers found.

200
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:45,029
The asker thinks about how hard it is to ask.

201
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:49,300
The asked person thinks about how hard it is to say no.

202
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:51,220
Nobody wants to say no to a face.

203
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:52,740
So they say yes.

204
00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:54,760
And they feel good about it.

205
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:56,960
So the asked person is also afraid, Ben.

206
00:08:57,500 --> 00:08:58,500
Afraid of saying no.

207
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:01,860
Two afraid people, and one box between them.

208
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:04,000
Two afraid people, Mia.

209
00:09:04,869 --> 00:09:05,860
And the box is light.

210
00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:07,260
That is the funny part.

211
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,400
Both are afraid of a face.

212
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:11,680
The box takes ten seconds.

213
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:13,620
The fear takes a year.

214
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:17,780
And when the ask finally happens, both people are relieved.

215
00:09:18,390 --> 00:09:20,360
The asker, because the face was kind.

216
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:22,680
The asked, because they got to help.

217
00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:25,920
Everybody wins, and the box comes down.

218
00:09:26,860 --> 00:09:27,959
They feel good about it.

219
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:30,540
Ben, that is the part I never saw.

220
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:32,700
I thought I was sparing my neighbor.

221
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:34,440
Saving him from a bother.

222
00:09:35,139 --> 00:09:36,720
But helping feels good.

223
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:40,380
When he got my box, he said yes and he smiled.

224
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:44,700
I was giving him a good minute, and I kept it from him for a year.

225
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:46,920
You were not sparing him, Mia.

226
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:48,400
You were starving him.

227
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:52,000
And one more number from that study, because it helped me most.

228
00:09:52,710 --> 00:09:56,880
The researchers asked the students, after everyone said yes, to guess again.

229
00:09:57,620 --> 00:09:58,720
And the guess went up.

230
00:09:59,580 --> 00:09:59,880
A little.

231
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:01,160
Not all the way.

232
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:02,820
They still guessed too low.

233
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:04,920
Still too low, Ben?

234
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,780
After ten yeses in a row?

235
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:08,850
How is that possible?

236
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:11,300
Ten yeses is a lot.

237
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:13,380
Still too low, Mia.

238
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:15,280
The picture is strong.

239
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:18,260
It only changes when the pile of yeses is big.

240
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:20,600
So one yes is not enough.

241
00:10:21,180 --> 00:10:21,940
You need many.

242
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:26,200
That is why the box was your first yes, and you need more.

243
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:28,840
And I got more, Ben.

244
00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:32,060
After the box, I asked the woman at the bakery to slice the bread.

245
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:32,940
Small.

246
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:34,140
She said yes.

247
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:36,500
Then a colleague, to look at my report.

248
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:37,460
Yes.

249
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:39,900
Then my sister, to keep my keys.

250
00:10:40,520 --> 00:10:40,940
Yes.

251
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:42,880
The pile is growing.

252
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:44,840
And the picture is changing.

253
00:10:45,620 --> 00:10:46,100
Slowly.

254
00:10:47,070 --> 00:10:50,880
"The pile is growing." Mia, that is exactly how it works.

255
00:10:51,980 --> 00:10:53,320
Okay, so that was part two.

256
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:55,520
Why asking feels so bad.

257
00:10:56,220 --> 00:10:58,720
We talked about Flynn and Bohns and the students.

258
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:02,120
We said people say yes twice as often as we think.

259
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:05,360
And we said the pile of yeses changes the picture.

260
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:07,340
Now, part three.

261
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:10,700
Simple ways to ask for help this week.

262
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:11,860
Mia, you go first.

263
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:13,670
Okay, part three.

264
00:11:14,420 --> 00:11:16,880
My first idea is the smallest ask, Ben.

265
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:21,700
Because some people listening want to ask, and do not know the words.

266
00:11:21,780 --> 00:11:22,280
Here it is.

267
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:26,240
"Could you help me with something small?" And then wait.

268
00:11:26,940 --> 00:11:27,460
That is all.

269
00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:29,960
Two sentences and a wait.

270
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:32,180
The wait is the hard part.

271
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:41,270
"Two sentences and a wait." Mia, my first idea is for English, because this is where I see the most people on chairs with brooms.

272
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:45,260
A learner hears a word and does not understand.

273
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:45,960
And they nod.

274
00:11:46,490 --> 00:11:48,480
They smile and nod.

275
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:51,640
And the conversation goes on, and they understood nothing.

276
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:55,020
I nodded for a whole year in Spanish, Ben.

277
00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:58,960
Smiling, nodding, understanding nothing.

278
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:01,800
A year of nodding.

279
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:02,520
What is the idea?

280
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:04,140
Ask, Mia.

281
00:12:04,970 --> 00:12:07,429
"What does that mean?" Four words.

282
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:09,480
And here is the study in English.

283
00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:12,860
Everybody thinks less of the nod.

284
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:14,670
Nobody thinks less of the question.

285
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:17,940
When you ask me a Spanish word, I love it.

286
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:19,940
I get to explain.

287
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,620
I feel useful.

288
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:23,720
People say yes to that question every time.

289
00:12:24,550 --> 00:12:26,080
You are helping me by asking.

290
00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:30,220
"You are helping them by asking." Ben, that takes the fear away.

291
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:32,380
So this week, five people.

292
00:12:32,990 --> 00:12:34,990
"What does that mean?" Five times.

293
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:36,260
Five yeses.

294
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:37,620
Count the faces.

295
00:12:38,300 --> 00:12:39,580
Count the faces, Mia.

296
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:42,960
I promise the faces will be fine.

297
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:44,850
My second idea is a rule for work.

298
00:12:45,710 --> 00:12:46,700
The small ask.

299
00:12:47,729 --> 00:12:53,110
"Can you look at this for two minutes?" "Can you show me where this is?" Here is the rule.

300
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:57,380
If it takes you an hour and them two minutes, ask.

301
00:12:58,140 --> 00:12:59,420
The math is not close.

302
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:02,540
An hour for me, two minutes for them, Ben.

303
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:04,960
I think I have ten of those every week.

304
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:07,420
What happens to the people I ask?

305
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:09,420
Do they get tired of me?

306
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:11,600
They get closer to you, Mia.

307
00:13:11,660 --> 00:13:14,640
That is what happened at my work, after the new person asked.

308
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:17,270
People started asking her things back.

309
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,070
She became the person everyone talked to.

310
00:13:20,780 --> 00:13:21,490
In three months.

311
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:23,090
Because she asked.

312
00:13:23,970 --> 00:13:25,200
Asking is not a cost.

313
00:13:25,880 --> 00:13:26,580
It is a thread.

314
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:30,740
Every ask ties two people together a little more.

315
00:13:30,790 --> 00:13:37,080
"If it takes you an hour and them two minutes, ask." Ben, that is your two years in one sentence.

316
00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:39,820
My second idea is about receiving.

317
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:42,120
Because there is another side of the box.

318
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:46,560
When someone says, "Let me know if you need anything," what do you say?

319
00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:49,240
"I am fine," Mia.

320
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:50,460
Always.

321
00:13:51,380 --> 00:13:54,040
When my father was sick, I did everything alone.

322
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,360
Hospital, work, home.

323
00:13:57,080 --> 00:14:03,900
And people said, "Let me know if you need anything." And I said, "I am fine." Thirty times.

324
00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:05,800
I said it to my sister.

325
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:07,360
I said it to my boss.

326
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:09,220
And I was not fine.

327
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,300
And "I am fine" was pushing them away, Ben.

328
00:14:12,940 --> 00:14:13,859
They wanted to help.

329
00:14:14,220 --> 00:14:15,320
They were waiting in the door.

330
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:17,560
What would you do now?

331
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:18,900
Now I say yes, Mia.

332
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:20,540
Not to everything.

333
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:21,960
To one thing.

334
00:14:22,950 --> 00:14:23,980
"Actually, yes.

335
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:29,690
Could you bring dinner on Thursday?" One real thing, with a day and a time.

336
00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:31,470
And they are relieved.

337
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:32,880
Relieved.

338
00:14:33,720 --> 00:14:39,730
Because "let me know" is a door, and they are standing in it, waiting for someone to walk through.

339
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:40,190
Anyone.

340
00:14:41,049 --> 00:14:46,120
"'Let me know' is a door, and they are waiting in it." Ben, that turns the whole thing around.

341
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:51,200
My year on the chair was a year of not giving my neighbor his best afternoons.

342
00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:53,020
I thought I was sparing him.

343
00:14:53,080 --> 00:14:54,020
I was starving him.

344
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:57,560
And I became the helper this year, Mia, and I learned something.

345
00:14:58,340 --> 00:15:00,659
My neighbor asked me to move a sofa.

346
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:02,540
I was busy, honestly.

347
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:03,280
And I said yes.

348
00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:05,120
And I moved the sofa.

349
00:15:05,810 --> 00:15:07,910
And I felt good for the whole afternoon.

350
00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:10,040
Better than the work I was doing.

351
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:13,180
You might be giving people the best part of their day.

352
00:15:13,190 --> 00:15:18,700
"You might be giving people the best part of their day." Ben, my third idea is for home.

353
00:15:19,340 --> 00:15:21,980
Because there is an ask at home that I always skip.

354
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:23,720
I come home tired.

355
00:15:24,010 --> 00:15:25,050
There is dinner to make.

356
00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:29,580
And I make it alone, angry, while my roommate sits on the sofa.

357
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:30,780
And she does not know.

358
00:15:31,660 --> 00:15:33,420
She does not know, Mia.

359
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:34,420
I have been the sofa.

360
00:15:34,980 --> 00:15:35,720
Many times.

361
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:38,800
I did not know my wife was drowning in the kitchen.

362
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:40,580
She thought I should see.

363
00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:42,100
I thought she was fine.

364
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:45,820
Two people, two pictures, one kitchen.

365
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:48,800
Two pictures, one kitchen, Ben.

366
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:50,540
And nobody says a word.

367
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:53,580
Why is it so hard to ask the people we live with?

368
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:56,080
Because we think they should know, Mia.

369
00:15:56,830 --> 00:16:00,090
"If he loved me, he would see." But nobody sees.

370
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:02,960
People see their own picture.

371
00:16:02,970 --> 00:16:07,690
The sofa looks like rest to the person on it, and like laziness to the person at the stove.

372
00:16:08,570 --> 00:16:10,610
The only way across is five words.

373
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:11,680
Ask.

374
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:14,760
The picture cannot cross the kitchen by itself.

375
00:16:15,690 --> 00:16:21,380
"Two people, two pictures, one kitchen." Ben, so now I say five words.

376
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:24,840
"Can you cut the onions?" Five words.

377
00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:27,420
And she says yes, and she jumps up.

378
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:28,180
Happy.

379
00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:34,079
Because the ask says, "Come and be in this with me." That is what asking really says.

380
00:16:35,090 --> 00:16:38,330
"Come and be in this with me." Mia, that is beautiful.

381
00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:44,360
My third idea is about the word "alone." Because I think it is the real topic under this one.

382
00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:48,700
We ask for help so rarely because we believe we should not need it.

383
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:51,920
Strong people do it alone.

384
00:16:51,960 --> 00:16:54,460
But strong people are just people with a broom, Ben.

385
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:55,600
On a chair.

386
00:16:56,180 --> 00:16:56,820
For a year.

387
00:16:57,570 --> 00:16:58,650
And they are not alone.

388
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:01,920
They only feel alone, because they are not asking.

389
00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:07,080
People are all around, on the other side of the wall, saying "of course."

390
00:17:08,169 --> 00:17:09,880
"People are not alone.

391
00:17:09,940 --> 00:17:16,840
They only feel alone." Mia, and one last idea, for the people who think their ask is different.

392
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:17,169
Bigger.

393
00:17:18,169 --> 00:17:21,110
"My problem is not a box." Money.

394
00:17:21,620 --> 00:17:21,970
Health.

395
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:23,580
A hard family thing.

396
00:17:24,380 --> 00:17:25,020
I understand.

397
00:17:25,729 --> 00:17:27,100
And I want to say two things.

398
00:17:28,060 --> 00:17:28,800
Please, Ben.

399
00:17:29,020 --> 00:17:29,820
The two things.

400
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:32,160
For the people with the big boxes.

401
00:17:32,600 --> 00:17:33,440
The heavy ones.

402
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:35,240
First, Mia.

403
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:38,100
The study was about strangers and small things.

404
00:17:38,760 --> 00:17:40,820
But the same picture works for big things.

405
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:43,240
You are guessing people at half.

406
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:44,740
They are at double.

407
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:48,140
Second, a big ask can start small.

408
00:17:48,890 --> 00:17:52,600
"Can I talk to you about something for ten minutes?" That is a box.

409
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:54,780
Everyone can lift that box.

410
00:17:55,620 --> 00:17:57,360
"You are guessing them at half.

411
00:17:57,660 --> 00:18:01,640
They are at double." Ben, that is our message today.

412
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:04,320
People say yes twice as often as we think.

413
00:18:04,790 --> 00:18:08,600
The asking feels terrible to us, and normal to them.

414
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:09,730
Helping feels good.

415
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:13,160
And "let me know" is a door with a person in it.

416
00:18:13,280 --> 00:18:14,330
So find your box.

417
00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:15,440
Knock on the door.

418
00:18:15,780 --> 00:18:16,640
Seven words.

419
00:18:17,080 --> 00:18:20,240
"Can you help me with a box?" And watch the face.

420
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:21,640
It will light up.

421
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:23,540
It will light up.

422
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:29,420
Mia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

423
00:18:30,740 --> 00:18:31,020
Ask.

424
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:32,620
To say what you need.

425
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:34,120
Ask for help.

426
00:18:35,100 --> 00:18:35,860
Ask a question.

427
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:37,220
Just ask.

428
00:18:38,500 --> 00:18:38,770
Help.

429
00:18:39,520 --> 00:18:41,639
To do something for someone.

430
00:18:41,700 --> 00:18:42,620
Help me with a box.

431
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:44,960
Helping feels good.

432
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:46,700
People.

433
00:18:47,480 --> 00:18:48,500
People say yes.

434
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:50,880
People are waiting in the door.

435
00:18:52,220 --> 00:18:52,560
Yes.

436
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:54,880
The answer we do not expect.

437
00:18:55,900 --> 00:18:56,640
They said yes.

438
00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:58,960
Twice as many yeses.

439
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:00,700
Box.

440
00:19:01,460 --> 00:19:02,040
My box.

441
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:03,840
Find your box.

442
00:19:05,320 --> 00:19:05,760
Ask.

443
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:06,760
Help.

444
00:19:07,480 --> 00:19:07,980
People.

445
00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:09,280
Yes.

446
00:19:10,120 --> 00:19:10,660
Box.

447
00:19:11,420 --> 00:19:13,280
Five words, and one box.

448
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:15,120
And one big idea.

449
00:19:15,720 --> 00:19:16,580
Ask for help.

450
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:19,680
Now, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?

451
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:20,780
Yes, Mia.

452
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:24,340
Everyone listening, here is my question.

453
00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:25,400
What is your box?

454
00:19:26,380 --> 00:19:29,400
The thing you have been reaching for alone, with a broom.

455
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:32,860
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

456
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:43,020
"My box is asking my boss for a day off." And then, if you want, add, "I will ask this week." We are on chairs, too.

457
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:45,040
We read every one.

458
00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:47,180
We are on chairs, too.

459
00:19:47,690 --> 00:19:52,000
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

460
00:19:52,060 --> 00:19:54,390
We make a new episode every few days.

461
00:19:54,840 --> 00:19:58,840
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

462
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:02,020
The full text of this episode is in the description.

463
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:06,260
Read it, and then ask someone what one word means.

464
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:09,000
Thank you for spending this time with us.

465
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:12,450
Maybe you are on a chair right now, with a broom, reaching.

466
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:14,080
Get down.

467
00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:15,780
Knock on the door.

468
00:20:16,940 --> 00:20:17,720
Seven words.

469
00:20:18,709 --> 00:20:22,100
"Can you help me with a box?" And watch the face.

470
00:20:23,140 --> 00:20:24,520
It will not do what you think.

471
00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:26,820
It will do the opposite.

472
00:20:27,620 --> 00:20:28,300
This is Mia.

473
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:29,540
And this is Ben.

474
00:20:30,220 --> 00:20:32,000
This was Two Cups English.

475
00:20:32,520 --> 00:20:33,400
Ask for help.

476
00:20:33,900 --> 00:20:35,120
We will see you next time.

